course: dictionary
lesson: 11881
title: Brakes: a word with two jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Brakes are the parts of a bike, car, train or other vehicle that slow it down or stop it. «Sam checked the brakes on his bike before he rode to school.» Here brakes is a noun: the parts themselves.
explain: Brakes can also be a verb. «The bus driver brakes gently at every stop.» Brakes is used with he, she or it, and it means she or he uses the brakes to slow down or stop the bus.
explain: Context helps you choose. Ask: is it a thing, or is someone doing an action? In «the brakes failed», the brakes are a thing. In «the cyclist brakes at the corner», someone is doing something. Try putting in "slow down or stop" to check.
explain: Brakes matter for road safety. «Squeeze both brakes gently and evenly.» Brakes work less well when the road is wet, so leave more space between you and the vehicle in front.
fact: Brakes are the parts that slow a vehicle down or stop it.
fact: «Brakes» can be a noun, the parts themselves, or a verb form, when someone uses them: «She brakes at the red light.»
fact: Brakes work by friction, which turns movement energy into heat.
fact: «Put on the brakes» means slow down or stop; «slam on the brakes» means stop very suddenly and hard.
fact: Brakes work less well on wet roads, so you need more distance to stop safely.
exercise: «Sam checked the brakes on his bike before he rode to school.» What are the brakes? | *The parts that slow a vehicle down or stop it | The bell that warns people | The wheels that move the bike forward | Bags carried on the back of a bike
exercise: «The bus driver brakes gently at every stop.» What does brakes mean here? | He or she sings loudly to the passengers | The bus has a new set of wheels | The bus is painted red | *He or she uses the brakes to slow down or stop
exercise: The road is wet. What should you do about your brakes? | Turn your brakes off to save them | *Leave more space, because brakes work less well when wet | Ride faster to warm them up | Brake hard and fast to be sure
test: «The brakes failed, so the lorry rolled down the hill.» What does brakes mean here? | *Parts of the lorry that slow or stop it | The road signs at the top | The trees along the hill | The lorry's horn
test: «Mia brakes when the traffic light turns red.» What does brakes mean here? | She buys a new bike | She speeds up | *She uses the brakes to slow down or stop | She cheers loudly
exam: Why should you leave more space when the road is wet? | Because wet roads are easier to ride on | Because brakes are not needed in the rain | To make your brakes stronger | *Because brakes work less well on wet roads
